  "notes": "West's lead of the ♠6 must be from a 4 card suit. Players conventionally lead their fourth best card from a broken holding. As the ♠6 is the lowest outstanding spot card in spades, West must have only 4 spades.\n\nSouth still has nothing to lose by holding up the ♠A until the second round. South has 8 top tricks (the ♠A, the ♥A, 2 diamond tricks, and 4 club tricks). South must decide whether to try for his ninth trick in hearts or in diamonds.\n\nSouth cannot afford to combine his chances in hearts and diamonds. If South cashes the ♦K and ♦A and finds diamonds breaking 4-1, it will be too late to play on hearts. The defense will be able to cash 2 spade tricks and 2 diamond tricks in addition to a heart trick.\n\nSome bridge problems can be solved only by using probabilities. 3 diamond tricks can be taken if that suit divides 3-2. A 3-2 break will occur about 68% of the time. An extra trick in hearts can be taken if West has either the ♥K or the ♥Q or both honours. West will have 1 or more heart honours about 76% of the time. It is, therefore, superior to play on hearts.\n\nSouth leads a low heart and when West follows low, so does dummy. East can win the trick and the defense will cash their spades.\n\nAfter regaining the lead, South leads another low heart. If West follows low, another finesse is taken. When East can only produce a low card, South has his ninth trick.\n\nBridge players should have a good working knowledge of probabilities of suit breaks and honour distributions. Many bridge books contain tables of important bridge odds. Memorizing a handful of numbers can help solve most problems like this one.",
